<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Content Library Category Remediation"
  summary="Human + AI collaboration to trace duplicate marketplace categories end-to-end, validate 400+ connector repos, and clean 21 stale AppDirect entries — 38 categories down to 17 in one session."
  date="March 2026"
  role="Investigation Lead / Remediation Owner"
  tags={["Human + AI", "AppDirect API", "Data Quality", "Marketplace", "Cursor"]}
>

<h2>Problem</h2>

<p>The internal Content Library showed duplicate and inconsistent category names in the filter panel — for example, "Communication" vs "Communication &amp; Collaboration", or "Endpoint Security &amp; Management" vs "Endpoint Management &amp; Security". The UI looked unpolished and made it harder for customers and internal teams to browse connectors by category.</p>

<p>Interestingly, the <strong>public marketplace looked clean</strong> while the <strong>internal library did not</strong> — a clue that the root cause was not only in connector source files.</p>

<h2>My Role</h2>

<p>I owned the investigation, root cause analysis, safety validation, and execution. AI (Cursor) handled scanning, scripting, and API enumeration at scale; I focused on decisions, verification, and what users would actually see after cleanup.</p>

<h2>What I Did</h2>

<h3>1. Traced category flow end-to-end</h3>

<p>Using Cursor, I followed how categories move through the system:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code>connector.yaml</code> metadata in each connector repo</li>
  <li>CI/CD publish pipeline</li>
  <li>AppDirect marketplace backend</li>
  <li>Content Library UI</li>
</ul>

<p>The canonical category list lives in <strong>turbine-sdk</strong> — that became the source of truth for what names should exist.</p>

<h3>2. Scale analysis across 400+ repos</h3>

<p>Instead of manually opening hundreds of repositories, AI helped write a script to compare <strong>last published</strong> vs <strong>current main</strong> across the connector org and surface mismatches.</p>

<p>I then queried the AppDirect marketplace API directly to enumerate all <strong>38 live categories</strong> and cross-referenced them against the canonical SDK list.</p>

<h3>3. Key discovery</h3>

<ul>
  <li>AppDirect <strong>stores categories historically</strong> and does not remove unused entries when connectors are republished</li>
  <li>Fixing source metadata and republishing (21 PRs plus CI/CD runs) would still <strong>not</strong> remove stale categories from what users see</li>
  <li><strong>Direct API cleanup</strong> was the only path to fix the internal Content Library UI</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Safe cleanup</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Reviewed all 38 live categories</li>
  <li>Verified all 21 duplicate/stale entries had <strong>zero connectors</strong> tagged to them</li>
  <li>Removed those entries via the AppDirect API</li>
</ol>

<h2>Constraints</h2>

<ul>
  <li>400+ connector repositories — manual inspection was not feasible</li>
  <li>Republishing alone would not fix historical AppDirect category accumulation</li>
  <li>API deletion required proof that no connectors were attached to a category before removal</li>
  <li>Work went beyond the Tech Pubs role — marketplace data quality with customer-facing UI impact</li>
</ul>

<h2>Outcomes</h2>

<h3>Customer and product impact</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>17 clean, canonical categories</strong> — zero duplicates in the Content Library</li>
  <li>Cleaner, more professional browsing experience</li>
  <li>Completed in <strong>one working session</strong> instead of an estimated multi-day cross-repo investigation</li>
</ul>

<h3>Process impact</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Established a repeatable pattern: trace flow → script at scale → validate via API → human safety checks</li>
  <li>Demonstrated when AI should do heavy lifting (scanning, scripts, API calls) and when a human must own validation</li>
</ul>

<h2>Human + AI split</h2>

<table class="path-table" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;">
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th>AI (Cursor)</th>
      <td>End-to-end code tracing, repo comparison scripts, AppDirect API enumeration, bulk analysis across 400+ repos</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Human (me)</th>
      <td>Canonical source decisions, zero-connector verification before deletion, stakeholder communication, safety gates</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Lessons Learned</h2>

<ul>
  <li>When public and internal surfaces disagree, trace the full pipeline before assuming the fix is at the source file</li>
  <li>Historical marketplace backends can accumulate stale metadata that republishing never cleans up</li>
  <li>Pairing with AI turns cross-repo investigations from days into a single focused session — if you validate every destructive step yourself</li>
</ul>

